You've hit on the exact right intuition: it depends entirely on which client you're using. Both httpx.Client and httpx.AsyncClient have the same chain (get → request → build_request → send) — the difference is just async/await sprinkled on every network-touching step. Let me redo the table properly.

Corrected table — split by client type
Stage	requests (Session)	httpx sync (Client)	httpx async (AsyncClient)
Sugar verbs	s.get(), s.post()	client.get() — sync	await client.get() — async def
Dispatcher	s.request()	client.request()	await client.request()
Build request obj	prepare_request() → PreparedRequest	build_request() → Request — sync, no await	build_request() — also sync, no await ⚠️
Real machinery	s.send()	client.send()	await client.send()
Mount resolution	get_adapter(url) inside send	_transport_for_url(url) — sync	same method — sync, no await ⚠️
Connection pool	adapter → urllib3 (sync)	transport → httpcore sync pool	transport → httpcore async pool (uses anyio)
Redirect handling	loop inside send()	sync loop inside send()	async loop with await per hop
Key clarification on your doubt
"you said there is no async .get in httpx"
That was about the module-level one-shot only:

python
Copy
httpx.get(...)           # ✅ exists, sync only
await httpx.get(...)     # ❌ does NOT exist — no async one-shot
await client.get(...)    # ✅ totally fine — AsyncClient.get IS async
Once you have an AsyncClient, everything is async — get, post, request, send, stream. The client type determines sync vs async, exactly as you guessed. The methods even mirror each other:

python
Copy
# httpx source — AsyncClient
async def get(self, url, ...):
    return await self.request("GET", url, ...)

async def request(self, method, url, ...):
    request = self.build_request(...)   # ← still sync! no await
    return await self.send(request, ...)

async def send(self, request, ...):
    ...
Note the two "surprise" sync methods on AsyncClient
build_request() is sync even on AsyncClient — it only merges headers/cookies/params into a Request object; no I/O happens, so there's nothing to await. (await client.build_request(...) would actually raise an error.)
_transport_for_url() is sync too — it's just a dict lookup over _mounts. Only transport.handle_async_request() (the actual bytes-over-the-wire part) is async.
Rule of thumb: anything that touches the network or the event loop is async; anything that's pure object construction/lookup stays sync — in both libraries.

Difference between request() and prepare_request() (requests)
These do different jobs and are often confused because both "build" something:

session.request(method, url, ...) — the full dispatcher
Takes raw user input, builds AND sends the request.
Internally it does: Request(...) → prepare_request() → send().
Returns a Response (network was actually hit).
Merges session state (headers, cookies, auth, hooks).
python
Copy
resp = s.request("GET", url)   # full pipeline: build + send
session.prepare_request(Request(...)) — build only, no send
Takes a requests.Request object, merges session state, returns a PreparedRequest.
No network I/O. Nothing is sent.
Gives you the exact final wire request to inspect/tweak/serialize before sending.
python
Copy
req = requests.Request("GET", url, headers={"X": "1"})
prepared = s.prepare_request(req)   # ← still nothing sent

print(prepared.url)        # final URL with params merged
print(prepared.headers)    # final headers after session merge
print(prepared.body)       # exactly what will go on the wire

resp = s.send(prepared)    # ← NOW the network call happens
Side-by-side
request()	prepare_request()
Input	method, url, kwargs	a Request object
Output	Response (already sent)	PreparedRequest (not sent)
Network I/O	✅ yes	❌ none
Session merge	✅	✅
Use case	normal calls	inspecting, modifying, caching, templates
Chain relationship
code
Copy
request()  =  prepare_request()  +  send()
             (build + merge)      (I/O)
httpx equivalent:

requests	httpx
s.prepare_request(req)	client.build_request("GET", url, ...)
s.send(prepared)	client.send(req) (or await)
Same split: build (sync, pure) vs send (I/O — sync in Client/Session, async in AsyncClient).
